   "source": "ROW = 0   # chosen pruning level (alpha = 0): see the discussion below\n\npruned = DecisionTreeClassifier(random_state=0,\n                                ccp_alpha=prune.loc[ROW, \"alpha\"]).fit(X_acute, y_acute)\n\nplt.figure(figsize=(12, 6))\nplot_tree(pruned, feature_names=X_acute.columns, class_names=pruned.classes_,\n          filled=True, fontsize=10)\nplt.title(\"Pruned decision tree\")\nplt.show()\n\nfor r in tree_to_rules(pruned, list(X_acute.columns)):\n    print(r)"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "**Choice: \u03b1 = 0 (6 leaves, CV accuracy 0.992).** The first pruning step (\u03b1 \u2248 0.098) removes only 2 leaves but drops the CV accuracy to 0.858, a loss of about 13 percentage points, and further pruning loses even more. Every split in the full tree is therefore needed for high accuracy, so cost-complexity pruning shows that the 6-leaf tree is already the minimal accurate tree. Its 6 rules (1(c)) are short and use only 4 of the 6 features, so they are already highly interpretable.",
   "id": "fb3ac19d"
  },
