  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "The highest-CV features are mostly **counts** (`NumStreet`, `NumInShelters`, `NumIlleg`, `NumImmig`, `NumUnderPov`, `population`, `numbUrban`, `HousVacant`) and **police features**. They are extremely right-skewed: most communities are near 0 with a few very large values, which is why their CV is high and why the box plots show many outliers.\n\n**Can we judge significance from the scatter plots alone?** No. Some plots suggest a weak positive association with violent crime, but each scatter plot shows only one feature against the target in isolation. Significance in a regression is about a feature's effect *given all the other features* and requires a formal test (e.g., coefficient p-values). Because many of these features are highly correlated with each other (e.g., counts that all grow with population), a feature that looks related on its own may add nothing once the others are in the model. A high CV also says nothing about predictive importance: it only measures spread relative to the mean, and the police features have high CV largely because of the median imputation.",
   "id": "ca7d1bd4"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "### 2(f)\u2013(j) Regression models\n\nAll models report the **test mean squared error (MSE)**. Hyperparameters are chosen by shuffled 5-fold cross-validation on the training set; shuffling matters because the rows are roughly ordered by state.",
